.TH VTPM-MDS 8 "September 2026" "vtpm-mds 0.1.0" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
vtpm-mds \- QEMU/Proxmox IMDS and TPM attestation service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vtpm-mds
.RB [ \-config
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-debug ]
.RB [ \-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vtpm-mds
serves EC2-IMDSv2-compatible instance metadata and TPM DevID enrollment
for QEMU guests. On a Proxmox lab host it listens on
.B 169.254.169.1:80
so the IMDS bridge (vmbr_imds) and nftables DNAT of
.B 169.254.169.254:80
reach this process. Empty
.I inventory_path
falls back to Proxmox
.IR /etc/pve .
.PP
Legacy command names
.B prox-mds
and
.B qemu-mds
are aliases of this binary.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-config " path"
YAML configuration file. The package installs
.IR /etc/vtpm-mds/config.yaml .
.TP
.B \-debug
Log the loaded configuration.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the package/build version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings live under
.B mds
in the configuration file.
.TP
.B require_vm_identity
Default
.BR true .
A caller whose connection the VM inventory could not bind to a VM record is
refused at the endpoints that report its instance identity:
.I /latest/meta\-data/instance\-id
and
.IR /latest/dynamic/instance\-identity/document .
The refusal is
.B 404
with the body an unknown route returns, so the caller cannot tell the two
apart; each one is logged with the caller's peer address, so an operator can.
The rest of the metadata tree,
.B PUT /latest/api/token
and
.I /health
are served to an unbound caller either way.
.IP
Setting it to
.B false
is a migration aid for a deployment whose inventory is incomplete: unbound
callers are then served an instance id synthesized from their peer address.
That id names the connection, not an instance, and is not an identity. Add the
missing guests to the inventory and remove the setting.
.SH EK PINS
On the first successful DevID enrollment of a VM without an inventory
.BR ek_sha256 ,
the service writes the SHA\-256 fingerprint of that VM's EK certificate to
.IR /var/lib/vtpm\-mds/ek\-pins/<vmid> .
Later enrollments of that VM with another EK are refused, and so is any
enrollment whose EK is pinned to a
.B different
VM ID, in this node's pin store or in another VM's inventory
.BR ek_sha256 .
The guest's
.B devid\-enroll
then fails with
.IP
HTTP 401: EK certificate is pinned to another VM
.PP
and the journal names both VMs, never the fingerprint:
.IP
devid enroll auth: EK certificate is pinned to another VM vm=<vmid> pinned=<other>
.PP
A finish that loses a race against another VM's first enrollment with the
same EK logs the same text with the prefix
.B devid enroll/finish:
instead. That finish has already consumed its enrollment session, so the guest
starts over with
.BR enroll/start .
.PP
Only pin files may live in
.IR ek\-pins/ :
any file whose name is a VM ID counts as that VM's pin, so a pin set aside
must be moved out of the directory, not renamed inside it. Files whose name
is not all digits are ignored.
.PP
.B Cluster pins.
On Proxmox the service also reads
.IR /etc/pve/vtpm\-ek/<vmid>
.RB ( cluster_pin_dir ),
written by root on a vTPM's first initialisation (a hook outside this
package); the service only reads it. A cluster pin is exactly 64 lowercase
hex characters, optionally followed by one newline: the
.B ek=
value of
.BR "devid issued" .
Anything else is no pin and logs
.BR "devid enroll: invalid cluster pin vm=<vmid>" .
A VM's own pin is its inventory
.BR ek_sha256 ,
else its cluster pin, else its local pin. A cluster pin that does not match
answers
.B 401
.IR "EK certificate does not match cluster pin" ;
beside a cluster pin no local pin is created, and a differing local pin logs
.BR "devid enroll: local pin differs from cluster pin vm=<vmid>" .
When
.I /etc/pve/vtpm\-ek
exists but holds no pin for the VM, each enrollment leg logs
.B "devid enroll: no cluster pin vm=<vmid>, using local pin"
and the local pin applies, unless
.B require_cluster_pin
is true: then the VM is refused with
.B 401
.I "no cluster pin for this VM"
and no local pin is created. Turn
.B require_cluster_pin
on only after every existing VM has a cluster pin.
.PP
The refusal of an EK pinned to another VM also counts every cluster pin, so
it holds on every node. A pin, local or cluster, of a VM ID that has no guest
configuration left on any node
.RI ( /etc/pve/nodes/*/qemu\-server/<vmid>.conf
or
.IR \&.../lxc/<vmid>.conf )
refuses nobody: a destroyed VM's vTPM may then enroll again under another ID.
.PP
The service never deletes or rewrites a pin; each case below is the
operator's.
.TP
.B A clone that kept its source's vTPM state
.B qm clone
copies the vTPM, so the clone presents its source's EK and is refused. Stop
the clone, remove its
.B tpmstate0
and create an empty one
.RB ( "qm set <vmid> \-\-tpmstate0 <storage>:1,version=v2.0" ),
then boot it; it gets its own EK and enrolls. A template that clones are made
from should carry an empty
.BR tpmstate .
.TP
.B A VM restored under a new VM ID
The restore keeps the vTPM, so the new ID is refused while the old VM still
exists. Once the old VM is destroyed its pins refuse nobody and the new ID
enrolls. With
.B require_cluster_pin
the new ID also needs a cluster pin: as root, write the
.B ek=
value of its EK as one line to
.RI "" /etc/pve/vtpm\-ek/ "<new vmid>" .
.TP
.B A reused VM ID
A new VM under the ID of a destroyed one has a new vTPM. The hook replaces
the cluster pin on its first initialisation; where it did not run, the VM is
refused with
.IR "EK certificate does not match cluster pin" :
delete
.I /etc/pve/vtpm\-ek/<vmid>
and, if present on this node,
.IR /var/lib/vtpm\-mds/ek\-pins/<vmid> .
.TP
.B A replaced vTPM
The VM's own pin no longer matches. Delete
.I /var/lib/vtpm\-mds/ek\-pins/<vmid>
on every node that has it, and its cluster pin unless the hook replaced it,
and enroll again.
.PP
A pin directory, local or cluster, that exists but cannot be listed, or a
cluster pin that cannot be read, makes every enrollment answer
.B 500
.IR "pin store unavailable" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/vtpm-mds/config.yaml
Daemon configuration (dpkg conffile).
.TP
.I /var/lib/vtpm-mds
Runtime state (JWKS, signing key).
.TP
.I /var/lib/vtpm-mds/ek-pins
EK pins, one file per enrolled VM ID (see
.BR "EK PINS" ).
.TP
.I /etc/pve/vtpm\-ek
Cluster EK pins, written by root, read by the daemon (see
.BR "EK PINS" ).
.TP
.I /lib/systemd/system/vtpm-mds.service
systemd unit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR devid-enroll (1)
